+# Model spec performance
+
+## Scope and reproduction
+
+This phase is limited to `spec/models`, with no application runtime changes,
+factory changes, parallelization, or changes to what examples assert. Requests
+are a future phase. System and streaming specs are excluded.
+
+Run `scripts/profile-models baseline` to run **only** `bundle exec rspec spec/models`
+with seed 12345, RSpec's top-20 timings, JSON results, and external wall-clock
+measurement. Results go to `tmp/rspec-profile/baseline/` (not version-controlled).
+Use `SEED=54321` to check a different order, and `STACKPROF=1` to sample execution:
+
+```sh
+STACKPROF=1 scripts/profile-models sampled
+bundle exec stackprof tmp/rspec-profile/sampled/stackprof.dump --text --limit 30
+```
+
+Keep sampling and ordinary timing runs separate when comparing improvements.
+RSpec execution time and total process wall time (including Rails boot) are both
+important. Always compare equal example counts and check for failures.
+
+## Initial observations
+
+- All examples currently reset Sidekiq to inline mode. Model setup can therefore
+  execute workers unrelated to the behavior under test.
+- Account factories already supply reusable signing keys; do not change factories
+  in this phase.
+- `stackprof` and `test-prof` are already available; no dependencies need adding.
+
+The measurements and confirmed findings below record the baseline and final results.
+
+## Baseline (seed 12345)
+
+Environment: Ruby 4.0.6, single RSpec process, local PostgreSQL/Redis, default
+filters (`js`, `search`, `streaming` excluded), no coverage instrumentation.
+
+| Run | Examples | Failures | RSpec execution | Process wall | User CPU | System CPU |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| Original, uninstrumented | 1,795 | 0 | 147.509 s | 150.424 s | 130.787 s | 8.015 s |
+
+Largest cumulative files: cleanup policies (13.374 s), trending statuses
+(12.374 s), tag feeds (11.579 s), statuses (11.284 s), public feeds (7.492 s),
+media attachments (6.935 s), and notifications (6.738 s). These largely create
+many accounts/statuses. Webhook workers serialize their objects even when no
+webhooks exist, multiplying setup work across these otherwise unrelated specs.
+
+A 35% improvement requires execution below 95.881 s (and process wall below
+97.776 s) for the same original examples.
+
+## Confirmed shared bottlenecks
+
+The sampled original run took 157.431 s execution / 160.289 s wall (1,795
+examples, zero failures). Sampling overhead means this is diagnostic, **not**
+the timing baseline used to claim an improvement.
+
+- `ActiveRecord::LogSubscriber#log_query_source`: 34.7% inclusive samples;
+  `Thread.each_caller_location`: 34.0%. A file-load-time
+  `ActiveRecord.verbose_query_logs = true` in the fetch-replies model spec leaks
+  into all subsequent examples (and even other types in a mixed run). Remove
+  the stray debugging assignment rather than suppressing warnings/errors or
+  changing application logging.
+- `TriggerWebhookWorker#perform`: 23.5% inclusive samples, of which 92.7%
+  are in `WebhookService#call`. Inline jobs turn routine account/status setup
+  into webhook serialization and additional queries. Model specs should enqueue
+  jobs by default, with explicit opt-in for assertions about worker effects.
+- These percentages overlap (workers also log SQL); do not add them together.
+- BCrypt cost is already reduced by the Devise test configuration. Account
+  factories already reuse RSA keys. Neither needs changing in this phase.
+
+## Implementation and coverage boundaries
+
+- Removed the global `verbose_query_logs` assignment from the fetch-replies
+  model spec. SQL logging is still available; only the accidental backtraces
+  are gone. No Rails application configuration was changed.
+- `spec/support/model_spec_execution.rb` wraps **only** `type: :model` examples
+  in `Sidekiq.testing!(:fake)`. Use `:inline_jobs` on an example or group when
+  the behavior under assertion requires workers to execute. Sidekiq's block API
+  removes the thread-local override on exit, including failures. Other spec
+  types still get the existing inline setup in `rails_helper.rb`.
+- Inline opt-ins are the email-subscription callback group, the admin
+  account-action email-delivery example, and the user email-confirmation group.
+  The latter deliberately covers both delivery **and non-delivery** assertions:
+  a negative delivery assertion should not pass merely because jobs are fake.
+- Existing job-enqueue matchers still inspect real fake queues, cleared by
+  rspec-sidekiq before each example. Database transactions, cache/Redis cleanup,
+  validations, callbacks, and all existing assertions remain unchanged.
+- Image/video/audio processing is still real in model specs, including metadata
+  assertions. No factories, application code, requests, system, or streaming
+  specs were edited.
+
+The first trial took 60.897 s execution / 63.780 s wall, but had three mail
+failures. It was diagnostic only. The tags above fix them without weakening any
+assertions.
+
+### Notes for future phases
+
+Profile requests separately with `bundle exec rspec spec/requests`, never both
+folders (or the whole suite) in one Bash command. Do **not** assume a fake-job
+default is safe for requests; audit side-effect and negative assertions first.
+The original request configuration is intentionally unchanged in this phase.
+
+If investigating factories later, first measure which associations are unused
+and how often records are recreated. The baseline model timing table identifies
+high-volume setup consumers. Do not skip model validations/callbacks, fake
+cryptography indiscriminately, or share mutable records across examples merely
+to improve a timing number.
+
+## Verified improvement
+
+| Run | Examples | Failures | RSpec execution | Process wall | User CPU | System CPU |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| Optimized, seed 12345 | 1,795 | 0 | 60.809 s | 64.330 s | 49.178 s | 6.119 s |
+
+This is **58.78% less execution time (2.43x speedup)** and **57.23% less total
+wall time (2.34x speedup)** than the uninstrumented baseline. The JSON reports
+have exactly the same example IDs, and neither report contains pending tests.
+Only model specs were run. These are local measurements, not a claim about a
+particular CI machine's absolute time.
+
+For optional Sidekiq mode/isolation checks while running the same model examples:
+
+```sh
+VERIFY_MODEL_EXECUTION=1 scripts/profile-models isolation-check
+```
+
+The diagnostic hook verifies the selected mode before every example and verifies
+that the thread-local override is removed after execution, even on failure. The
+block API is thread-local: future tests that enqueue jobs from additional Ruby
+threads must explicitly manage those threads' modes instead of assuming the
+example's override propagates.
+
+Additional verification:
+
+| Run | Examples | Failures | RSpec execution | Process wall | User CPU | System CPU |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| Optimized, seed 54321 | 1,795 | 0 | 62.182 s | 65.085 s | 50.603 s | 5.809 s |
+
+The second seed also executed exactly the baseline's example IDs with no pending
+examples. A focused `VERIFY_MODEL_EXECUTION=1 bundle exec rspec spec/models
+--require ./docs/profile_models.rb --tag inline_jobs --seed 54321` passed all four
+mail examples, including the no-welcome-mail case (1.240 s execution).
+
+Lint passed for every changed/new Ruby file, and `bash -n scripts/profile-models`
+passed. Invalid output labels are rejected before tests run.
+
+The final diagnostic run (`VERIFY_MODEL_EXECUTION=1 STACKPROF=1`) passed all
+1,795 examples, with per-example mode and restoration checks enabled: 63.641 s
+execution / 66.652 s wall. `TriggerWebhookWorker#perform` fell from 23.5% to 0.1%
+inclusive samples; SQL query-source backtrace collection disappeared from the
+samples. Remaining large costs are ordinary database setup and real media work,
+useful starting points for the later factory phase.
+
+An ignored temporary failure probe selected six mail/subscription examples
+using `--example-matches '(EmailSubscription|mark_email_as_confirmed)'`, deliberately
+raised once in an inline example's before hook, and confirmed cleanup after the
+failure. Only that expected failure occurred; the other five examples passed.
+Rerunning the same six normally passed. This checks mode switching in both
+normal and inline examples, as well as the failed-setup path, without adding
+intentional failures to the repository's specs.
diff --git a/docs/profile_models.rb b/docs/profile_models.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5d7a7fb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/profile_models.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@
+# frozen_string_literal: true
+
+# Optional execution-only sampling: STACKPROF=1 scripts/profile-models baseline
+if ENV['STACKPROF'] == '1'
+  require 'stackprof'
+
+  RSpec.configure do |config|
+    config.before(:suite) do
+      StackProf.start(mode: :wall, interval: 1_000)
+    end
+
+    config.after(:suite) do
+      StackProf.stop
+      StackProf.results("#{ENV.fetch('MODEL_PROFILE_OUTPUT')}/stackprof.dump")
+    end
+  end
+end
+
+# Optional isolation checks, registered before the normal support hooks so this
+# around hook encloses the model-specific Sidekiq override.
+if ENV['VERIFY_MODEL_EXECUTION'] == '1'
+  RSpec.configure do |config|
+    config.around(:each, type: :model) do |example|
+      previous_mode = Sidekiq::Testing.__local_test_mode
+      begin
+        example.run
+      ensure
+        raise 'Model spec leaked its Sidekiq mode' unless Sidekiq::Testing.__local_test_mode == previous_mode
+      end
+    end
+
+    config.before(:each, type: :model) do |example|
+      expected_mode = example.metadata[:inline_jobs] ? :inline : :fake
+      raise "Expected Sidekiq #{expected_mode} mode" unless Sidekiq::Testing.__test_mode == expected_mode
+    end
+  end
+end
diff --git a/docs/summarize_model_profile.rb b/docs/summarize_model_profile.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..17e9dac
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/summarize_model_profile.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,15 @@
+# frozen_string_literal: true
+
+require 'json'
+
+# Usage: ruby docs/summarize_model_profile.rb tmp/rspec-profile/baseline/rspec.json
+report = JSON.parse(File.read(ARGV.fetch(0)))
+puts report.fetch('summary_line')
+puts "RSpec execution: #{report.fetch('summary').fetch('duration').round(3)}s"
+puts "\nTop files by cumulative example time:"
+report.fetch('examples')
+  .group_by { |example| example.fetch('file_path') }
+  .map { |file, examples| [file, examples.sum { |example| example.fetch('run_time') }, examples.size] }
+  .sort_by { |_, duration, _| -duration }
+  .first(20)
+  .each { |file, duration, count| puts format('%<duration>7.3fs %<count>4d examples %<file>s', duration:, count:, file:) }
diff --git a/scripts/profile-models b/scripts/profile-models
new file mode 100755
index 0000000..bccf3dc
--- /dev/null
+++ b/scripts/profile-models
@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@
+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+# Profile only the model specs; never expand this to the whole suite.
+set -euo pipefail
+cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."
+label="${1:-models}"
+if [[ ! "$label" =~ ^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]+$ ]]; then
+  echo 'Use a label containing only letters, digits, underscores, or hyphens.' >&2
+  exit 1
+fi
+output="tmp/rspec-profile/$label"
+mkdir -p "$output"
+export RAILS_ENV=test
+export MODEL_PROFILE_OUTPUT="$output"
+TIMEFORMAT=$'real %3R\nuser %3U\nsys %3S'
+set +e
+{ time bundle exec rspec spec/models \
+  --seed "${SEED:-12345}" \
+  --require ./docs/profile_models.rb \
+  --format progress --out "$output/rspec.txt" \
+  --format json --out "$output/rspec.json" --profile 20; } 2> "$output/time.txt"
+status=$?
+set -e
+cat "$output/time.txt"
+tail -85 "$output/rspec.txt"
+if [[ -s "$output/rspec.json" ]]; then
+  ruby docs/summarize_model_profile.rb "$output/rspec.json"
+fi
+exit "$status"
